Mock now refuses to use a buildroot that another Mock process is using,
instead of joining it.  Previously a second run that did not clean the
chroot first (e.g. `--shell`, `--chroot`, `--install`, `--copyin` or
`--no-clean` while a build ran) went ahead and, when it finished, killed
the other run's processes in the chroot as leftovers; when `mknod` is not
permitted (e.g. in a container) it also left device bind mounts behind, so
the chroot could not be deleted.  The second run now exits with code 60;
wait for the first run to finish, or use `--uniqueext` to work in a
separate buildroot ([issue#1836][]).
